# Doc 14 section 11.4 -- the logrotate substitution for WinSW's `roll-by-size-time`.
# Installed at /etc/logrotate.d/buybox by debian/postinst.
#
# `copytruncate`, not `create`: the service holds its log files open via systemd's
# `StandardOutput=append:`/`StandardError=append:`, and neither the app nor its systemd unit
# knows how to reopen that descriptor on a signal (no SIGHUP handler in `packages/shared/src/
# logger.ts`). `create` would rename the file and start a new one while the process kept writing
# to the renamed file's now-orphaned descriptor forever. `copytruncate` copies the content out
# and truncates the original in place, so the same open descriptor keeps working. Its one known
# cost is a few lines possibly lost in the copy/truncate race, judged acceptable because
# `app_events` (doc 05 section 10) is the record an operator actually searches; the file log's
# own ranking below it is already stated in doc 14 section 5 step 7's table.
/var/log/buybox/*.log {
  daily
  maxsize 10M
  rotate 30
  missingok
  notifempty
  compress
  delaycompress
  copytruncate
}
